meta {
  name: Get Public Lead Form (Unknown)
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/public/lead-form/zzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz
  body: none
  auth: none
}

tests {
  test("returns 404 for a token that never existed", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(404);
  });

  test("says nothing about whether the token ever existed", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().message).to.equal("This form is no longer available.");
  });

  test("leaks no agency or producer information", function () {
    const raw = JSON.stringify(res.getBody());
    expect(raw).to.not.contain("agencyName");
    expect(raw).to.not.contain("producer");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /public/lead-form/:token — unknown token (negative case)

  Documents the **non-disclosure contract** of the public routes, which is why
  it is a request in its own right rather than a note on the happy path.

  A well-formed but unknown token returns exactly what a revoked link returns,
  what a malformed token returns, and what a link belonging to a suspended
  agency returns:

  ```json
  { "message": "This form is no longer available.", "error": "Not Found", "statusCode": 404 }
  ```

  Identical status, identical body. Someone probing the endpoint cannot tell:
  - whether a token ever existed,
  - whether a link was revoked or never minted,
  - which agency (if any) a token belongs to,
  - whether the producer behind it is still active.

  Any future change that specialises one of these messages — however helpful it
  looks — reintroduces an oracle. The e2e suite pins this by deep-comparing the
  unknown, revoked and malformed responses to each other.

  Note the token here is 43 characters, so it passes the shape check and reaches
  the database lookup. A malformed token short-circuits earlier and must still
  produce this same response.
}
